{"id":"19ed06ee-d01a-4d73-8897-75dd194e4a58","object":"chat.completion","created":1785019703,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\n  \"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    nodes = case['nodes']\\n    required_symbols = set(case['required_symbols'])\\n    node_by_id = {n['id']: n for n in nodes}\\n    children = {n['id']: [] for n in nodes}\\n    for n in nodes:\\n        pid = n['parent']\\n        if pid is not None:\\n            children[pid].append(n['id'])\\n    retained = set(node_by_id.keys())\\n    order = sorted(retained, key=lambda nid: (-node_by_id[nid]['depth'], nid))\\n    removed = []\\n    for nid in order:\\n        if nid not in retained:\\n            continue\\n        node = node_by_id[nid]\\n        if node['parent'] is None:\\n            continue\\n        if node['symbol'] in required_symbols:\\n            continue\\n        if any(cid in retained for cid in children.get(nid, [])):\\n            continue\\n        new_retained = retained - {nid}\\n        present_symbols = {node_by_id[rid]['symbol'] for rid in new_retained if rid in node_by_id}\\n        if required_symbols.issubset(present_symbols):\\n            retained = new_retained\\n            removed.append(nid)\\n    retained_ids = sorted(retained)\\n    return {\\\"removed_node_ids\\\": removed, \\\"retained_node_ids\\\": retained_ids}\"\n}","reasoning_content":"We need to implement solve(case) as per the task description. The task is \"grammar_guided_node_reduction\". The payload contains nodes (a tree) and required_symbols. The objective is to reduce the tree by removing removable nodes (removable: true) while ensuring that all required_symbols are still represented somewhere in the retained tree. The process: deeper nodes before shallower, never remove root, required symbol nodes, or nodes with retained children. Tentatively remove only if all required symbols remain represented. Use node ID as stable tie-break at equal depth. Return sorted retained IDs and actual removal order.\n\nWe need to output removed_node_ids (order of removal) and retained_node_ids (sorted). The function should not mutate case.\n\nThe input case has nodes list with fields: id, parent, symbol, depth, removable, utility (utility not used? but maybe for ordering? The instructions say \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" and \"Use node ID as the stable tie break at equal depth.\" So we need to decide removal order: deeper nodes first, within same depth sort by node ID (presumably lexicographically or string compare). But note: \"Consider deeper parse-tree nodes before shallower nodes.\" That means we process nodes in descending depth order. Within same depth, sort ascending by node ID? Or descending? Tie break: \"Use node ID as the stable tie break at equal depth.\" Usually ascending order ensures deterministic. But we need to decide order of removal: for each node considered (in that order), we check if it's removable and if removing it (and its subtree) would still leave all required_symbols in the tree (considering that we might have removed some nodes already). If yes, remove it. After removal, we continue with remaining nodes. The removal order is the order in which we actually remove nodes. The retained node IDs are all nodes that are not removed, sorted (presumably by ID string).\n\nImportant: \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" Means we cannot remove a node if any of its children are retained (i.e., not removed). So when we tentatively remove a node, we must also consider its descendants: if we remove a node, its entire subtree is removed (since tree reduction). But we cannot remove a node that has retained children – meaning if a child is not removed (maybe retained for some reason), then the parent cannot be removed. However, in this process we are considering removals of nodes, and we might remove a parent before its children? But the instruction says \"deeper nodes before shallower nodes\" – so we consider children first, then parents. So when we consider a node, its descendants have already been processed (either removed or retained). If any descendant is retained (i.e., not removed), then the current node cannot be removed because it has retained children. If all descendants have been removed, then the node is a leaf in the current tree (or has only removed children), and we can consider removing it if it's removable and the symbol condition holds.\n\nSo algorithm:\n\n- Build a dict from id to node object.\n- Build parent-child relationships.\n- Determine initial set of required symbols (set). Also root is required implicitly? The root symbol \"root\" is in required_symbols. So we need to ensure root is never removed.\n\n- We need to simulate removal. We'll maintain a set of retained node IDs (initially all nodes). Also we need to track which symbols are present in retained nodes (i.e., symbols of retained nodes). Initially all symbols of all nodes are present.\n\n- Order nodes for consideration: sort by depth descending, then by id ascending (string compare). This gives processing order: deepest first, then tie break by id ascending.\n\n- For each node in that order:\n    - If node is not retained (already removed) -> skip.\n    - If node is root (id 'root' or parent null) -> skip (cannot remove).\n    - If node.symbol is in required_symbols -> skip (cannot remove).\n    - Check if any child of this node is retained. If yes -> skip (has retained children).\n    - Tentatively remove: compute what would happen if we remove this node. Removal means we remove this node and all its descendants (but since we are processing deeper first, all descendants should have been processed already and might have been removed or retained. But if any descendant is retained, then this node would have retained children and we skip. So all descendants are already removed. So we just need to check if the removal of this node would cause a required_symbol to be missing. Since we remove only this node, we need to check if its symbol is required? But we already skipped required symbols. However, a required symbol might be present in a descendant that is already removed. But if that descendant was removed, then that symbol is already missing. But we need to ensure that after removal, all required symbols are still represented in retained nodes. So we need to compute the set of symbols in retained nodes after removal. But we have to consider that we might have removed other nodes before, and this removal would further remove this node's symbol. But since we skip if the node's own symbol is required, and we also skip if it has retained children (which would retain its symbol possibly), the only concern is that this node might be the only retained node with a particular required symbol? But we already skip if required symbol. So it's fine? Actually, required symbols may be present in multiple nodes. We need to ensure that after removing this node, every required symbol still exists in at least one retained node. Since we skip nodes whose own symbol is required, the node's symbol is not required. So removal of this node does not directly remove a required symbol. But it could be that a descendant (already removed) had a required symbol, but that's gone. However, required symbols must be present in retained nodes. So we need to check that after removal, each required symbol is still present in the set of retained nodes (including this node if we don't remove it). So before removal, we check: if there is any required symbol that is only represented by this node (i.e., its symbol is not required, but maybe another required symbol is only present in this node's descendants that were already removed? That can't happen because we skip if any descendant is retained. So all descendants are removed. So the only symbol contribution of this node is its own symbol. Since its symbol is not required, removing it doesn't affect required symbols. However, could it be that this node is the only node with a required symbol that is not its own symbol? No, a node's symbol is its own. So it seems the condition \"all required symbols remain represented\" is automatically satisfied if we skip required symbol nodes and nodes with retained children. But the instruction says \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" So we must verify.\n\nConsider scenario: required symbol \"s1\". There is a node with symbol \"s1\" that is removable. But we skip because symbol is required. So we never remove it. Good. If required symbol appears only in a node that is not root and not required symbol? It is required symbol, so we skip. So the only way a removable node could affect required symbols is if removing it removes a node that had a required symbol as a descendant (but that descendant is also required and we wouldn't remove it because we skip required symbol nodes). Since we process deeper first, a child that is required symbol would be processed before its parent. That child would be skipped (cannot remove). So when we get to parent, it has a retained child (the required symbol node) -> skip because has retained children. So parent is not removed. So condition holds.\n\nThus the condition might be redundant for typical cases, but we must still check. So algorithmically, we need to compute required symbols and check that after removal, each required symbol appears in at least one retained node.\n\nImplementation steps:\n\n1. Parse case: case is dict with keys \"nodes\" and \"required_symbols\". Also maybe \"_case_nonce\" but ignore.\n2. Build a list of nodes. Each node is dict with id, parent, symbol, depth, removable, utility (ignore utility). We need to know parent references. Use a dict node_by_id.\n3. Build children map: for each node, add to parent's children list if parent is not None.\n   root is the node with parent None.\n4. Determine required symbol set: set(required_symbols).\n5. Initialize retained set = set of all node ids.\n6. Determine processing order: sort node ids by depth descending, then by id lexicographically ascending (string compare). Use node_by_id to get depth.\n7. For each node_id in that order:\n   - if node_id not in retained: continue\n   - node = node_by_id[node_id]\n   - if node['parent'] is None (root): continue (cannot remove)\n   - if node['symbol'] in required_symbols: continue (cannot remove)\n   - # Check if any child is retained: if any child_id in retained: continue\n   - # Tentative removal: compute new retained set = retained - {node_id} (and all its descendants? but descendants already removed if we are processing deeper first; but we removed them earlier, so they are already out of retained. So only remove node_id.\n   - # Check required symbols: For each req_sym, check if there exists some retained node (in new retained set) with that symbol. If any missing, continue.\n   - # If passes, remove: remove node_id from retained set, and record removal order (append node_id to removed list).\n8. After loop, removed_node_ids = list in order of removal. retained_node_ids = sorted list of retained set (ascending string order).\n9. Return {\"removed_node_ids\": removed_node_ids, \"retained_node_ids\": retained_node_ids}.\n\nEdge cases: If no removable nodes, removed_node_ids empty, retained all.\n\nWe need to be careful: The processing order includes nodes that are already removed. But we skip them. Also, when we remove a node, its parent may later be considered (since parent depth is shallower, comes later). That parent might have no retained children (since all children removed) and can be removed if conditions met.\n\nOne nuance: The description \"Never remove ... a node with retained children.\" This implies we cannot remove a node if any of its children are still retained. Our algorithm respects that.\n\nAlso \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" We compute after removal.\n\nUse only standard library. No randomness.\n\nWe'll implement solve(case) accordingly.\n\nNow produce the JSON with implementation. The implementation should be a Python function. Make sure to handle missing keys? But case is always as given.\n\nWe must produce exactly one JSON object with key \"implementation\". The value is a string containing the Python source code, properly escaped.\n\nLet's write the code.\n\nNote: The output schema requires \"removed_node_ids\" and \"retained_node_ids\" as arrays of strings.\n\nWe'll write:\n\ndef solve(case):\n    nodes = case['nodes']\n    required_symbols = set(case['required_symbols'])\n    node_by_id = {n['id']: n for n in nodes}\n    children = {n['id']: [] for n in nodes}\n    for n in nodes:\n        pid = n['parent']\n        if pid is not None:\n            children[pid].append(n['id'])\n    retained = set(node_by_id.keys())\n    # order: depth descending, then id ascending\n    order = sorted(retained, key=lambda nid: (-node_by_id[nid]['depth'], nid))\n    removed = []\n    for nid in order:\n        if nid not in retained:\n            continue\n        node = node_by_id[nid]\n        if node['parent'] is None:  # root\n            continue\n        if node['symbol'] in required_symbols:\n            continue\n        # check retained children\n        if any(cid in retained for cid in children.get(nid, [])):\n            continue\n        # tentative removal\n        new_retained = retained - {nid}\n        # check required symbols still present\n        if not all(any(node_by_id[rid]['symbol'] == sym for rid in new_retained if rid in node_by_id) for sym in required_symbols):\n            # Actually we need to check each required symbol exists in new_retained\n            # Simpler: compute symbols present in new_retained\n        present_symbols = set(node_by_id[rid]['symbol'] for rid in new_retained)\n        if required_symbols.issubset(present_symbols):\n            # proceed removal\n            retained = new_retained\n            removed.append(nid)\n    retained_ids = sorted(retained)\n    return {\"removed_node_ids\": removed, \"retained_node_ids\": retained_ids}\n\nBut careful: after we remove a node, we should also remove its descendants? But we already processed deeper nodes first; if a descendant was not removed, it would be retained, and that would block parent removal because of retained children. But if we remove a parent, we should also remove its descendants (they are no longer part of tree). However, our algorithm as described removes only the node itself, but its retained descendants would remain in retained set. That would violate the parent removal because we check retained children. So we cannot remove a parent if it has retained children. So if we hypothetically remove a parent, we would also need to remove its children (but those children may have been retained). But the instruction \"Never remove ... a node with retained children.\" means we only remove a node if all children have been removed already. So we never remove a parent that still has retained children. Therefore, when we remove a parent, its children are already removed (since we process deeper first and they were either removed or retained; but if they were retained, we wouldn't remove the parent). So the children are already in removed set or retained set? If they were retained, then we would not remove the parent. So we don't need to cascade removal of children because they are already gone. However, consider a scenario where two siblings: one is removed, one is retained (due to required symbol). Then parent cannot be removed. Good.\n\nBut what if a child is removed, but its descendant is retained? That can't happen because if descendant is retained, the child would have retained children and would not be removed. So consistency.\n\nThus our algorithm stands.\n\nMake sure to iterate over order list that includes all original node IDs, but we skip if not retained. That's fine.\n\nWe'll implement.\n\nNow produce the JSON with implementation string.\n\nWe need to escape newlines and quotes. In Python, we can use json.dumps to create the JSON string. But we need to output only the JSON object. So we'll write the code as a string and then enclose.\n\nLet's produce the final answer."},"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop"}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":2172,"completion_tokens":3476,"total_tokens":5648,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":2048},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":3142},"prompt_cache_hit_tokens":2048,"prompt_cache_miss_tokens":124},"system_fingerprint":"fp_8b330d02d0_prod0820_fp8_kvcache_20260402"}